        "lesson": "phy9-c1-s2-n19",
        "locale": "en",
        "edition": "modern-us-neutral-en",
        "text": "19. Gas Properties Used in Engineering\n\nThe compressibility of gases, their adjustable pressure, and their response to temperature changes are useful in many engineering devices.\n\nState equation (2-19) can explain many of the gas properties discussed in this lesson.\nHere we use the ideal-gas approximation;\nit does not describe real gases at every pressure, temperature, or phase.\n\n**The elastic action of compressed gas**\nWithin the ideal-gas approximation, the pressure of a gas on the container walls is\n\n$p=\\frac{m}{\\mu}\\cdot\\frac{RT}{V}$.\n(2-23)\n\nConsider the formal limits $m\\to0$, $V\\to\\infty$, or $T\\to0$ separately in this model, keeping the other relevant quantities appropriately finite;\nthe pressure approaches zero.\n\nAs a gas becomes more rarefied, the pressure on the walls can become very small, but pumping out gas does not mean removing every molecule.\nThe low-temperature limit also goes beyond the range of the classical ideal-gas model;\nthe last limit must not be interpreted as saying that the molecules of real matter necessarily become completely motionless at absolute zero.\n\nThe force of a gas on a wall is $F=pS$, where the area is that of the surface receiving the force, and the pressure on that surface is assumed uniform.\nCompressing a confined gas generally raises its pressure, producing resistance to compression somewhat like a spring.\n\nUnlike a solid spring, a gas does not retain a fixed shape;\nit occupies the available space connected to it.\nThis analogy does not mean that a gas follows a linear spring law in every process.\n\n**Adjusting gas pressure**\nChanging a gas volume or temperature can change its pressure.\n\nFor the same gas species, if volume and temperature remain fixed, pressure is also proportional to the gas mass (see 2-23).\nAdding gas to a container or releasing some of it can therefore adjust the pressure.\n\nInflating a car tire or a soccer ball is a familiar example, although inflation can also change the volume and temperature.\nEquipment must operate within its material-strength limits and permitted conditions;\nan ideal-gas formula does not imply that a container can withstand arbitrary pressure.\n\nCompression pumps and compressors can compress gas and supply it to a container.\nA piston compressor has basic structural similarities to a common piston water pump, but not all compressors have the same detailed construction.\n\n**Gas compressibility**\nAt ordinary temperatures and pressures near atmospheric pressure, gases are easier to compress than liquids and solids.\n\nThe same pressure change will often produce a more noticeable volume change in a gas.\nHowever, the pressure change also depends on the initial volume, the amount of gas, and the temperature conditions of the process;\na large volume change does not always produce only a small pressure change.\n\nFigure $26$ shows an isotherm for a fixed amount of gas.\nWhen the initial volume $V_1$ is larger, the same decrease in volume $\\Delta V$ corresponds, within the range shown, to a smaller pressure increase $\\Delta p_1$.\n\nWhen the initial volume $V_2$ is smaller, the same decrease in volume $\\Delta V$ corresponds to a larger pressure increase $\\Delta p_2$.\nIf a confined gas is compared with a compressed spring, its resistance to compression is therefore not constant.\nAlong this isotherm, greater compression makes the pressure increase caused by the same further compression larger.\n\nA gas can do work on its surroundings by pushing a movable boundary such as a piston;\nwhen a gas is compressed, its surroundings can do work on it.\n\nA gas can change its volume relatively easily, allowing it to transmit force over a longer stroke.\nOnly with suitable temperature conditions, a large gas reservoir, a continuous gas supply, or comparable conditions can the pressure change during operation be small.\n\n\n\nFigure 26\n\nGas compressibility allows a larger amount of gas to be stored in a purpose-built pressure vessel for storage and transport.\nThis use must respect the permitted pressure and temperature of the vessel;\nan ordinary bottle is not equivalent to a pressure vessel.\n\n**Temperature effects on pressure and volume**\nFor a fixed amount of gas, heating at fixed volume raises the pressure;\nheating at fixed pressure increases the volume.\n\nThese correspond respectively to the laws called Charles's law and Gay-Lussac's law in the original textbook;\nthe fixed-volume and fixed-pressure conditions must not be combined.\n\nUsing the pressure or volume at Celsius zero as the reference, and retaining the textbook ideal-gas approximation, the pressure temperature coefficient $\\gamma$ and volume expansion coefficient $\\alpha$ are written as\n$\\gamma=\\alpha=\\frac{1}{273}$ degrees$^{-1}$.\n\nThe original textbook describes these coefficients as several hundred times larger than the corresponding coefficients of liquids and solids;\nthe actual ratio depends on the materials and temperature range being compared.\n\n**A small amount of gas can produce appreciable pressure**\nAt a suitable volume and temperature, a small mass of gas can still produce appreciable pressure.\n\nIn an ordinarily rarefied gas, molecular attraction is insufficient to keep the molecules packed closely together, so they can move within the container.\nMolecules collide with the container walls and exchange momentum, producing gas pressure.",
